{
	title: 'Clients report their local timezone',
	spec: `Terminal and web clients report the local IANA timezone of the device they run on, read with Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone, when they connect. The host treats it as untrusted input: it accepts only a bounded string that Intl accepts as a timezone and that is not a numeric UTC offset, and ignores anything else. The value is never logged or written to tracked files. The zone of the client that last gave a session input is saved in that session (session.ason timezone) and is the fallback when no connected client has a zone, so a host restart does not switch a resumed turn to the host zone.

The host exposes, for a session, the timezone of the client that most recently sent input to that session, falling back to a connected client currently viewing it, then the zone saved in the session, otherwise nothing. A terminal client running over SSH reports the remote shell's zone, which usually equals the host's, so a UTC-like zone (UTC, Etc/*, GMT) is no evidence of the user's location.

The field is \`timezone\` on the tab-start command (terminal and web) and on tab-resume (a web page opened at a tab's address); the host's clients registry (src/host/clients.ts) keeps it on the connection record and remembers per session which record last sent a submit or answer. Keep it to that one small field; no location services, IP lookup or new dependency. A focused check covers accepting a valid zone, ignoring an invalid one, and per-session lookup of the most recent sender.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'A host restart resumed turns before clients reconnected; with no client zone the prompt used the host zone (UTC), then the reconnecting client switched it back, adding two false Session diff notes. Invariant: a session keeps its last sender zone across restarts.'
	],
	needs: []
}
